Aerie-Purpose: TEST ONLY. Make the browser process hold 768 MB so the perf gate has a regression to catch.
Aerie-Kind: test-only (never listed in patches/series, never part of a release build)

Applied on demand by `python3 -m aerie_bench.testpatch apply TREE` (docs/bench.md, "Proving the
gate trips"), never by ./aerie build. The browser process allocates 768 MB at startup and touches
every page, so memory_after_ad_pages_mb rises by about 768 MB over the same build without it.
chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main_linux.cc is touched by no Helium or Aerie patch, so no layer
reads or rewrites it: the hunk applies on a tree whose layers are applied, and a later
./aerie build (which re-applies layers only) does not undo it. ./aerie build --clean does.

--- a/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main_linux.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/chrome_browser_main_linux.cc
@@ -69,6 +69,12 @@
 #endif  // !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS) && BUILDFLAG(USE_DBUS)
 
 void ChromeBrowserMainPartsLinux::PostCreateMainMessageLoop() {
+  // AERIE BENCH TEST ONLY. Holds 768 MB of touched memory for the life of the
+  // browser process, so the perf gate has a regression to catch.
+  static char* const aerie_bench_memory_hog = new char[768u << 20];
+  for (size_t i = 0; i < (768u << 20); i += 4096) {
+    aerie_bench_memory_hog[i] = 1;
+  }
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
